# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-windows-drive-protocol-three-evals-fails — cad-drive-protocol-actions-three-evals-counter-aware fails on the Windows runner
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: portability
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

=test:alfe:windows= (native Windows runner, =allow_failure: true=), job
16756197957 on master pipeline 2885678935: the suite completes with 1208 checks
and *4 failures, all in one test* —
=CAD-DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS-THREE-EVALS-COUNTER-AWARE=, on
=(EVAL-RESULT-STATUS RESULT)=.

The same test passes on Linux (SBCL and CCL) and on macOS.

* Context that matters

The immediately preceding run of that lane (job 16755546814) was WORSE: it
aborted mid-suite inside =PROTOCOL-MOCK-CAD-FULL-LIFECYCLE= with an unhandled
condition and reported nothing at all. So the lane was already red before
2026-09-26's work; what changed is that it now runs to completion and isolates
the failure to one test, which is what makes this ticket possible.

* Why it is probably platform, not logic

The test drives the FILE protocol against the mock: three =:eval= actions, each
waiting for =DONE N= with N the next request counter. It is exactly the shape
that Windows file semantics disturb, and the repository already carries two
findings of that class:

- =write-atomic-file='s overwriting rename is NOT atomic on Windows (UIOP does
  delete-then-rename), so a reader can transiently see the target missing --
  see =write-atomic-file-concurrent-rename-windows= (closed) and the Windows
  skip in =protocol-write-atomic-file-contention=.
- Readers must use =:if-does-not-exist nil= and never probe-then-open, because
  the delete gap of a hot-rewritten file makes TOCTOU fire.

So the first hypothesis is that the mock's =status.txt= / =stdout.txt= updates
are read across such a gap, and =eval-result-status= comes back NIL (or stale)
for one of the three actions rather than =:ok=.

* Directions

1. Get the 4 failing checks' actual values from the job trace (the names are
   there; the values are truncated in the summary) -- which of the three
   actions, and what status came back.
2. Decide between the two honest outcomes, and do not fudge the difference:
   - the ASSERTION is platform-dependent (a status legitimately observable as
     pending on Windows) -> relax it for Windows the way the contention test
     does, with the reason at the call site; or
   - the PROTOCOL DRIVER has a real race that Windows merely exposes -> fix
     the driver, since a real CAD on that platform would hit it too. This is
     the outcome that matters, and it is the reason this is not simply skipped.
3. Only then consider =:skip-on-os=; a skip chosen before step 1 would hide
   the second outcome.

* Diagnosed and fixed — clautolisp 2.2.122

Step 1 of /Directions/ answered the question: the failing check is
=(EVAL-RESULT-STATUS RESULT)= evaluating to *=:ABORTED=*, not to a stale
=:SUCCESS=. =:ABORTED= is set in exactly one place
(=backend-cad-common.lisp=, =wait-done=): =wait-for-status-prefix= did not
match within the request timeout. So it is a GIVE-UP, not the
stale-status race the ticket's first hypothesis proposed -- and the fault is in
the TEST MOCK, not the driver.

=SPAWN-MOCK-CAD-RUNTIME= was a =DOTIMES= over =cycles=, and each round waited
for =stdin.txt= for at most *two seconds*, then published =RUNNING n= / =DONE n=
*anyway* and moved on. Two consequences:

1. a slow round CONSUMED the budget, so with =:cycles 3= and three actions the
   third request was never acknowledged and the driver timed out -- which is the
   =:ABORTED= the Windows lane reported;
2. a =DONE= published for a request that never arrived is a status the driver
   could match against a send it has not yet made -- latent nondeterminism on
   every platform, not only Windows.

The mock now SERVES =cycles= requests instead of SPENDING =cycles= rounds: the
counter follows the requests actually served, so it cannot run ahead of the
driver, and the only cap is one overall deadline (20 s) whose job is to stop a
mock nobody feeds -- its test then fails on its own assertion instead of hanging.
Every one of the six call sites sends exactly =:cycles= actions, so this is what
they all meant.

*Reproduced on Linux before fixing*, which is what the ticket asked for rather
than a platform-specific guess: =cad-mock-serves-a-request-that-arrives-late=
sends its action 3 seconds after READY -- past the old per-round budget -- and
against the old mock it fails with

: a late request must still be acknowledged; got :ABORTED

the same symptom, the same word, on a machine with a fast filesystem. With the
fix it passes, and the echo assertion proves the request was really consumed
rather than answered by a status the mock had run ahead to publish.

alfe suite 1232 checks, 0 failures on SBCL.

* Acceptance criteria

- [X] *A test that fails on Linux too, so the fix is not platform-folklore* --
      =cad-mock-serves-a-request-that-arrives-late=, red with the old mock and
      with the same =:ABORTED=.
- [X] *Nothing was skipped.* No =:skip-on-os=, no relaxed assertion: the mock's
      contract was wrong and is now right on every platform.
- [ ] =test:alfe:windows= green on the runner. The symptom it reported is now
      impossible for the reason diagnosed above; whether it was the ONLY reason
      is what that lane will say. It is =allow_failure: true=, so it gates
      nothing meanwhile.

* The lane answered, and there was a SECOND cause (clautolisp 2.2.125)

=test:alfe:windows= ran on master at 2.2.124 (job 16758224382) and *failed* --
on the very test added to reproduce the first cause:

#+begin_example
CAD-MOCK-SERVES-A-REQUEST-THAT-ARRIVES-LATE
  a late request must still be acknowledged; got :ABORTED
Did 1247 checks.  Pass: 1245 (99%)  Fail: 2 ( 0%)
#+end_example

(The two entries are the same failure: the suite runs twice per SBCL process.)

So the per-round budget was NOT the only reason, exactly as the criterion below
asked the lane to say. The second cause is in the mock's read, and it is the
Windows anti-pattern this repository already knows:

#+begin_src lisp
(loop until (probe-file stdin) ...)          ; wait
(when (probe-file stdin)
  (setf request (read-file-as-string stdin)) ; <- probe, THEN open
  ...)
#+end_src

The driver publishes =stdin.txt= with =WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE= -- write a temp,
rename over -- and on Windows that rename is *delete+rename* (UIOP's
overwrite). Between a =PROBE-FILE= that succeeds and the =OPEN= that follows,
the file can be absent, or still held by the renaming party. And that read was
*the one operation in the cycle =%MOCK-RETRY-FILE-OP= did not wrap* -- its own
docstring describes this transient and says that hitting it "aborts the WHOLE
mock thread, so it stops consuming stdin.txt". Which is what happened: the mock
died, nothing consumed the request, and the driver timed out.

** Why it said nothing

The mock's body is guarded by design (an unhandled error in a thread under
=--disable-debugger= aborts the whole test process), but the guard was
=IGNORE-ERRORS= -- it *discarded* the condition. So a dead mock and a slow one
were indistinguishable at the assertion: =:ABORTED=, no cause. That is the real
reason this took a week rather than a run.

** Fixed

- =%MOCK-CONSUME-REQUEST= replaces the wait-then-probe-then-read with ONE
  loop: attempt the read (=:if-does-not-exist nil=, sharing violations caught),
  treat absence AND emptiness alike as "not yet", stop at the deadline. The
  only way to know a file is readable is to read it. An empty read is left
  alone on purpose -- it is what a half-written file looks like, and consuming
  it would strand the request for good.
- =%CALL-RECORDING-MOCK-CONDITION= replaces the bare =IGNORE-ERRORS=: still
  swallows (it must), but *records* into =*MOCK-CAD-CONDITION*=, and
  =%MOCK-CAD-FAILURE-NOTE= puts it in the failure message. If this lane goes
  red again it will name its cause instead of printing =:ABORTED=.
- Two tests: =cad-mock-consume-request-never-probes-then-opens= (absent,
  half-written, complete -- the empty-file case is the one that discriminates)
  and =cad-mock-death-is-recorded-not-swallowed=.

*A portability trap, recorded because the first version of the test fell into
it.* That second test first arranged a REAL failure -- delete the workdir so
the mock's first write signals. It passed on SBCL and *failed on CCL*, where
that write does not signal. So the guard was factored into a function and the
test now exercises the mechanism the thread actually runs, on both hosts. A
test that depends on which host makes an operation fail is testing the host.

alfe suite: *1278 checks, 0 failures, on SBCL AND CCL.*

*Still not proven*: that this was the LAST cause. The hypothesis is strong (the
symptom, the unwrapped read, the silent guard, and the helper's own docstring
all agree) but it cannot be run here -- reproducing an absent-file transient
needs the platform whose rename is not atomic. The lane will say. What IS now
guaranteed regardless of whether the hypothesis is right: the next red run
names its condition.

* RESOLVED 2026-09-27 — the lane ran the test and it PASSED (clautolisp 2.2.134)

=test:alfe:windows= on the =2.2.133= child (job 16762247996) reports:

#+begin_example
Did 1308 checks.  Pass: 1307 (99%)  Fail: 1 ( 0%)
Failure Details:
  AN-UNWRITABLE-DIRECTORY-DECLINES-RATHER-THAN-FAILS-THE-RUN
#+end_example

*=cad-mock-serves-a-request-that-arrives-late= is not in that list.* The test
written to reproduce this ticket's defect — the one that failed on Windows at
2.2.124 with =got :ABORTED= — now passes there. So both causes are confirmed
fixed ON THE PLATFORM, not only reasoned about:

- the mock's per-round budget (2.2.122), and
- the probe-then-open read whose transient killed the mock thread silently
  (2.2.125), which is the one that could only be settled by a platform whose
  rename is not atomic.

Criterion 3 asked for the LANE to be green. It is not, and deliberately not
counted against this ticket: the single remaining failure is a different test
with a different cause (a fixture whose premise does not hold on Windows), now
tracked as =empty-drawing-decline-test-premise-fails-on-windows=. Keeping this
ticket open until an unrelated test goes green would misreport what is fixed.

Closed. What this ticket set out to explain is explained, and what it set out to
fix is fixed and observed.

